Rule: php-xss-concat-assign
Pattern: User input appended into an HTML/echo buffer via .= without sanitization. Use htmlspecialchars($VAR, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8').
Code context:
<?php

header ("X-XSS-Protection: 0");

// Is there any input?
if( array_key_exists( "name", $_GET ) && $_GET[ 'name' ] != NULL ) {
	// Feedback for end user
	$html .= '<pre>Hello ' . $_GET[ 'name' ] . '</pre>';
}

?>

findings.remediation:
The vulnerability exists because user input from the `name` parameter is directly concatenated into HTML output without sanitization, allowing attackers to inject malicious scripts. To fix this, use `htmlspecialchars()` to encode special characters before outputting user input. Here's the corrected code:

```php
if( array_key_exists( "name", $_GET ) && $_GET[ 'name' ] != NULL ) {
    $html .= '<pre>Hello ' . htmlspecialchars($_GET[ 'name' ], ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8') . '</pre>';
}
```

